    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The MATLAB scripts shapecurve.m and diffGprofile.m were developed using the concepts of differential geometry to provide tools to examine quantitatively the curvature of geological surfaces. Two joint surfaces are examined with a focus on specific morphologies and structures. The main joint surfaces of both samples are approximately planar with local hyperbolic, parabolic and elliptical undulations. Hackle and cross fractures on both samples display similar curvatures despite significant differences in size. Rib marks on the second sample show distinct and similar morphologies, with concave bases and convex peaks. These analyses are presented as an example to encourage future studies using these tools on other surfaces of all scales. Other examples include stratigraphic horizons deformed by faulting (Chimney Rock, Utah and Wytch Farm, England) and by folding (Emigrant Gap, Wyoming).",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
